# KNOWN GAP — Core #8 SOUNDNESS: an UNDEFINED TYPE NAME is silently accepted
# in 9 of 10 type positions. Measured at HEAD 2026-08-17.
#
# The resolver CAN reject undefined types — the LOCAL-BINDING position proves
# it: `Zorblatt s = "hello"` is correctly rejected with 1 error. Every OTHER
# position accepts garbage:
#   - function parameters and return types
#   - struct fields
#   - tuple elements
#   - every generic argument
#   - Callable types
#
# Measured on this file: `gg check` -> "OK: no semantic errors".
# `gg run` on the struct-field cell -> the backend emits broken C and the user
# sees a raw `C compiler exited with: exit status: 1`, which
# docs/language-design.md:120 already forbids ("errors should point to source,
# not generated C"). In other cells the scout measured programs that BUILD AND
# RUN SUCCESSFULLY with an undefined type.
#
# Not `str`-specific: `Zorblatt` is an arbitrary name that resolves to nothing.
# This is the ROOT CAUSE of examples/md2html.gg's five-month invisible
# breakage (it uses the removed `str` keyword and nothing complained).
#
# Because ONE position already rejects, this is a MISSING CALL at 9 write
# sites — a Core #4 chokepoint fix, not 9 independent repairs.
#
# INTENDED: every type position rejects an unresolvable type name, with the
# same diagnostic the local-binding position already produces.
# Wired test: known_gap_undefined_type_name_rejected (tests/integration.rs,
# #[ignore]d).
struct Holder:
    Zorblatt field

int takes(Zorblatt x):
    return 7

void main():
    print(takes(3))
